 2. Right tries to pull Trump away from Big Tech 
 
 By Maria Curi 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Photo: Nicholas Kamm/AFP via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Prominent conservative thinkers and MAGA figures are seizing on former Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang's affair with an alleged Chinese spy to pull President Trump away from his alliance with the tech right. 

 Why it matters: The Nvidia scandal highlights some of the political weaknesses for Trump in allowing himself to be defined by his connections to an increasingly unpopular group of companies.

 Right-wing players are hoping the bombshell revelations about Huang's affairs will chip away at the president's embrace of the tech industry and offer an opening for anti-tech voices in his administration. What they're saying: They view the midterm elections as proof that people across the political spectrum hate AI and data centers and view Silicon Valley as too powerful. 

 Trump's perception of China "has always been entirely commercial and transactional. His view was, 'We're getting screwed. I'm going to get a better deal,'" said Oren Cass, founder of conservative think tank American Compass. Cass, who has warned about Nvidia risks for more than a year, said the allegations of espionage are the strongest evidence yet that China is not a commercial partner but an adversary that is going to "subvert the American political process in ways that end up humiliating the president." MAGA figure Steve Bannon, meanwhile, said in a text he has called Huang "a CCP 'Agent of Influence' a thousand times" and that it's an "OPEN SECRET." "We need to actually cut OFF ALL THE CHIPS NOW," Bannon added as Congress warms up to legislation aimed at that. What's next: Conservatives are hoping the scandal changes who gets a seat at the table for the remainder of Trump's presidency as 2028 and mounting AI unpopularity approach. 

