 3. Britain brings its AI pitch to Silicon Valley 
 
 By Ina Fried 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Britain wants a bigger piece of the AI boom without becoming wholly dependent on American technology, the country's AI minister told Axios ahead of a visit to Silicon Valley this week.

 Why it matters: The U.K. is trying to position itself as both an attractive destination for U.S. AI investment and an independent voice on how the powerful technology should be developed and regulated.

 Its approach offers a contrast to the European Union's sweeping AI legislation and the largely hands-off approach favored by the Trump administration. Driving the news: U.K. AI minister Kanishka Narayan is visiting the Bay Area this week for meetings with U.S. government counterparts, AI startups and leading AI companies. He is also scheduled to speak tomorrow at Stanford, where he earned his MBA.

 "Britain is back in business when it comes to AI," Narayan told Axios in an interview Friday. He said Britain wants a larger share of AI hardware and manufacturing, both to create British jobs and to strengthen the resilience of the U.S. and U.K. technology supply chains. The big picture: Rather than attempting to recreate the entire AI industry domestically, Britain wants to build strength in areas where it can gain leverage while relying on a diversified group of allies for other technologies.

